# Notebooks are stored without their outputs.
#
# Your working copy keeps them -- run the code, look at the results -- but what
# git records is the code you wrote.  Two reasons.  Outputs are where notebook
# merge conflicts come from, so stripping them keeps `git pull` from turning
# into an unreadable mess every time an assignment is revised.  And your code is
# what is being marked: I run your notebook, rather than reading a picture of
# what it printed on your laptop.
#
# The filter itself lives in .git/config and does not travel with a clone, so
# note/getting-set-up.ipynb sets it up.  If `git add` starts refusing a notebook,
# that is this filter telling you it is not configured -- section 6 of that
# notebook is the fix.
*.ipynb filter=nbstripout

# Readable notebook diffs, once `uv run nbdime config-git --enable` has run.
*.ipynb diff=jupyternotebook
*.ipynb merge=jupyternotebook
